WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.809 --> 00:00:03.080
Let's play a game, shall we?

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:10.240
The Saw series is one of the most bloody successful
horror franchises of all time, and Jigsaw's

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.740
work is still far from "game over."

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:19.119
Saw: Legacy will splatter 2017 with some all-new
trap-happy mayhem dealing.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:24.140
So, to prepare for the gruesomeness still
to come, here's a look at the untold truth

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:29.679
of Saw and how it became such a beloved gorefest
in the first place.

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:33.300
Keep watching, or don’t — make your choice.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.550
Cheap tricks

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:40.070
Like a lot of budding auteurs, Leigh Whannell
and James Wan had almost no money to make

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:45.360
their first movie together, so they had to
think cheap when coming up with the bare bones

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:46.960
of their fright flick.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.960
And what’s more budget-friendly than an
entire movie about two guys stuck in a single

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.080
room together?

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:58.070
At first, they thought of having their characters
stuck in an elevator with the whole movie

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:04.500
made up of the security footage -- a concept
which might’ve prevented M. Night Shyamalan’s

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.740
Devil from ever happening, but who knows.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:13.920
Wan even conceived of a Paranormal Activity-like
home footage concept before that series came

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.970
to be, but ultimately they settled on the
dank bathroom setting that would become so

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:22.250
iconic to the series.

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:23.679
First blood

00:01:23.679 --> 00:01:29.850
To court investors, Whannell and Wan made
a short film on a shoestring budget that showcased

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.399
the duo's originality and money-saving savvy.

00:01:33.399 --> 00:01:38.499
In it, Whannell starred as the character who
has to rifle around in a living friend’s

00:01:38.499 --> 00:01:43.139
intestines to find the key to the reverse
bear trap on his head, and that role would

00:01:43.139 --> 00:01:48.990
eventually be played in the full film by actress
Shawnee Smith, whom James Wan had a major

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.780
crush on since he was a kid.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:56.429
The spec also featured a Billy puppet made
by Wan himself, using ping-pong balls, paper

00:01:56.429 --> 00:02:01.729
towel tubes, and papier mâché, and the design
was so creeptastic that they didn’t bother

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:04.049
changing it for the full film.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:05.499
Head case

00:02:05.499 --> 00:02:11.920
The idea for John "Jigsaw" Kramer, the mastermind
behind most of the series' sinister games,

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.370
stemmed from a surprising source: Whannell's
own cranial treatment.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:21.209
The writer had been experiencing migraines
and when he went through some testing, he

00:02:21.209 --> 00:02:26.950
imagined what it would be like to get the
bad news of his own impending expiration date

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:28.280
and how someone might react.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:33.260
He might not have had a medical issue, but
his mind was definitely twisted.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.510
Show me the money

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:41.860
After Saw proved to be a surprise runaway
hit at the box office — grossing $103 million

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:49.299
worldwide against a reported $1.2 million
budget — actor Cary Elwes was pretty displeased

00:02:49.299 --> 00:02:53.770
with the comparatively small slice of the
pie he received from the proceeds.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:59.579
In a suit filed against the production companies
and producers, Elwes alleged that he was only

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:06.840
paid about $55,000 in all, even though he
was promised at least 1% of the producer profits.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:12.719
He sought half a million in damages, and the
parties eventually settled out of court.

00:03:12.719 --> 00:03:18.049
The acrimony must not have lasted long, because
Elwes came back to the franchise to reprise

00:03:18.049 --> 00:03:21.560
his Doctor Gordon role a few films later.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.670
"Congratulations Doctor Gordon, you survived."

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.000
Desperate for the sequel

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:34.930
Given the massive success of the first movie,
Lionsgate was in a rush to get Saw II moving

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:40.000
right away, and the producers thought outside
of the bathroom box for the second installment.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.609
Up-and-comer Darren Lynn Bousman had already
written a screenplay for his own film called

00:03:45.609 --> 00:03:52.770
The Desperate, but it was criticized for being
too similar to Saw, so producers saw an opportunity

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:57.670
to tinker with Bousman's flick and transform
it into a follow-up for their own.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:04.069
His foray into the Saw series was so successful
that he remained on board as a director of

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:07.209
the third and fourth films as well.

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:08.590
Sticking point

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:13.590
Although nearly all of the traps in the Saw
series were complicated, none could be more

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:18.590
labor-intensive than the needle pit scene
from Saw II.

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:25.520
120,000 needles were needed to fill the prickly
vat, and it took a round-the-clock team four

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:32.090
days to replace the real needles with fiber
optic tips for Shawnee Smith’s safety.

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:38.470
After all that prep making the site poke-free,
a crew member accidentally knocked a pile

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:44.080
of real needles back into the heap, so they
had to halt production before it could even

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.930
begin to locate them.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:50.410
Director Bousman later joked on Reddit that
"it was like finding a needle in a needle

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:51.410
stack.”

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:52.410
Good one.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:53.690
Good blood

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:59.419
As a nod to all the onscreen bloodshed in
the first two Saw films, Saw III's theatrical

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:06.550
promotion included a poster made using a real
vial of actor Tobin Bell's blood, mixed with

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:12.020
red ink to create the most visually jarring
shade for his cloak in the image.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:18.430
Lionsgate printed 1,000 copies of the design
and auctioned it off to benefit the Red Cross,

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:23.490
in addition to hosting a series of blood drives
organized by the studio that raised thousands

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:26.050
of pints for the organization.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:27.509
Toilet humor

00:05:27.509 --> 00:05:33.970
There's very little levity to be seen onscreen
in the Saw series, but behind the scenes,

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:37.190
the cast and crew found ways to keep the mood
light.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:41.550
One running gag during production was the
use of a hidden fart machine that was set

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:46.010
off during particularly difficult scenes,
just to mess with the actors:

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:56.450
"Hahahaha, hahahaha. Look, he's still acting!"

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:58.150
That's not all.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.569
During Bell's nude morgue scene, his splayed
out exposure got him "cupped" in the nether

00:06:03.569 --> 00:06:04.800
regions by co-stars.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.910
Talk about your crude humor.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:12.020
Friendly favors
Rather than rebuild the bathroom set from

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:17.400
the first film for Saw III, the filmmakers
simply called in a favor to the makers of

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:24.800
Scary Movie 4 and used their replica … minus
the basketball hoop and falling pipe, of course.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:25.800
"Damnit"

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:26.800
Oopsie daisy

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:31.990
In 2010, one Massachusetts theater full of
children was supposed to show an innocent

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:37.990
screening of the animated flick Megamind,
but that wasn't the film that actually started

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:38.990
to roll.

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:45.960
Instead, it was Saw 3D.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:51.120
Some of the children supposedly experienced
nightmares after seeing the first few minutes,

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.849
which is understandable considering the opening
scene has two men who have to hacksaw their

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:59.530
shared girlfriend to save themselves.

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:01.270
Sleep tight, kids!

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:02.509
Thanks for watching!

00:07:02.509 --> 00:07:06.509
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:07:06.509 --> 00:07:10.649
And leave us a comment to let us know which
Saw story you liked most...

